nodecode-apple-health is a rewrite of davidmosiah's davidmosiah/apple-health-mcp:

  https://github.com/davidmosiah/apple-health-mcp
  commit 94c31b8545e0d82a2d2e0eb1cb43da0e470b6d1f
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 David Mosiah (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the connector's model-facing tools and the shapes they answer with -
connection status, data inventory, the daily and the weekly summary with their
per-day numbers (steps, distance, active energy, heart-rate average/min/max,
resting heart rate, HRV, sleep hours, the day's workouts), the bounded record
list with a type filter, a day window and a truncation note, the bounded
workout list, and the privacy audit - plus the privacy modes (structured and
raw) and the HealthKit type prefixes the export writes. The original is an MCP
server in TypeScript served over stdio to the agent; here the export is parsed
in Common Lisp, one XML element at a time, so the cell needs neither Node, nor
npm, nor a subprocess, and the tool arguments are declared once as contracts so
the schema the model is shown and the body that runs are one shape. One thing
the original did not have: a /health slash command that answers the same
numbers as text.

Left out: the connector's setup and cache half, which has no file of its own to
work on here - `setup --auto-import`, which scans the usual download, desktop
and document folders for an export and copies it under a private directory,
`--watch-path`, which re-imports a newer export dropped in a folder, the
incremental parsed cache and the tool that clears it, `reimport`, the shared
wellness profile at ~/.delx-wellness/profile.json and the tool that updates it,
the synthetic demo answer, and the connection check that reads the agent's own
config file. Also left out: reading a .zip export (unzip it first), and the
timezone conversion of each timestamp into a requested zone - the cell groups
by the local day the export itself wrote.
